-- Article pages for the journal cards that had none. Idempotent: only fills posts whose body is still empty,
-- so articles edited in the admin are never overwritten.

UPDATE posts SET body_html = '<p>Ask a Bhutanese guide to name the country''s most beautiful building and many will answer without hesitation: Punakha Dzong. It sits on a spit of land where two rivers meet, its whitewashed walls and gilded roofs doubled in the water below. It is also a working monastery and seat of government, which is why the best visits are slow ones. Here is what to know before you walk across the bridge.</p>
<ol class="article-list">
<li><span class="article-list-number">01</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>A Fortress Built On A Promise</h3>
<p>Punakha Dzong was completed in the late 1630s under Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal, the lama who first unified Bhutan. It served as the seat of government until the capital moved to Thimphu in the 1950s, and it remains the winter home of the central monastic body and its Je Khenpo, the chief abbot. Fire, flood and earthquake have damaged it more than once; each time it was restored, which is part of why the building feels so alive rather than preserved.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">02</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Where Two Rivers Meet</h3>
<p>The dzong stands at the confluence of the Pho Chhu and the Mo Chhu, the &ldquo;father&rdquo; and &ldquo;mother&rdquo; rivers. Approach on foot across the long wooden cantilever bridge and the whole fortress opens up slowly across the water. Stand on the far bank at the start and end of the day when the light is soft and the colour of the river changes from milky turquoise to deep green.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">03</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Courtyards, Temples And The Utse</h3>
<p>Inside, three courtyards step up toward the utse, the central tower. The first courtyard holds the administrative offices, the second the monastic quarters, and the third the great assembly hall with its enormous carved pillars and painted murals. Your guide will explain the iconography as you go &mdash; the wheel of life, the protector deities, the stories from the life of the Buddha. Some rooms are open only at certain hours, so a local guide makes a real difference.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">04</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>When To Go</h3>
<p>March to May and October to November are the clearest seasons. In late spring the jacaranda trees in the courtyard flower a soft violet against the white walls, a sight that has become one of Punakha''s signatures. If your dates allow, the Punakha Drubchen and Tshechu in late winter bring masked dances and processions to the courtyards. Dates follow the lunar calendar and shift each year, so check before you plan.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">05</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Visiting Well</h3>
<ul class="tour-highlights tour-highlights--stack"><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Dress modestly: shoulders and knees covered, and a jacket for the cool courtyards</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Remove shoes and hats before entering any temple room</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Photography is welcome in the courtyards but not inside the shrine rooms</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Allow at least two hours, and add time for the walk across the bridge and along the river</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Visit with a licensed guide: access to certain halls depends on the day''s rituals</li></ul></div></li>
</ol>
<p>Punakha is easy to pair with Dochula Pass, the Chimi Lhakhang fertility temple and a gentle walk through the rice terraces at Sopsokha. Our <a href="/tours/royal-capital-immersion" class="faq-contact-link">Royal Capital Immersion</a> journey builds the dzong into a slow, private day, and we are always happy to tailor the route around your dates.</p>', seo_description = 'Why Punakha Dzong is often called Bhutan''s most beautiful fortress: its history, the river confluence, the spring jacarandas, visiting etiquette and the best time to go.', updated_at = datetime('now')
  WHERE slug = 'punakha-dzong-bhutan-s-most-beautiful-fortress' AND body_html = '';

UPDATE posts SET body_html = '<p>A tshechu is the high point of the Bhutanese year: a multi-day religious festival in which monks and laypeople perform sacred masked dances in the courtyard of a dzong, while families from surrounding villages arrive in their finest dress. For a traveler it is the closest thing to stepping into a living tradition. Here is how to plan one well, and how to watch it with the respect it deserves.</p>
<ol class="article-list">
<li><span class="article-list-number">01</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>What A Tshechu Is</h3>
<p>The word means &ldquo;tenth day&rdquo;, the day of the lunar month associated with Guru Rinpoche, the saint who brought Buddhism to Bhutan. Each district holds its own tshechu on dates set by the lunar calendar. The dances, known as <em>cham</em>, are meditative rituals as much as performances: each mask and gesture carries a meaning, and many Bhutanese believe that simply witnessing them brings a blessing.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">02</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>The Festivals Most Visitors Choose</h3>
<p>Paro Tshechu in spring is the best known, and it ends with the unveiling of a huge appliqu&eacute; thangka at dawn. Thimphu Tshechu in autumn is the capital''s great gathering. Bumthang''s festivals are quieter and more intimate, while smaller district festivals, among them Haa and the Jomolhari mountain festival, offer a village atmosphere with far fewer visitors. Each has a different character, so it is worth choosing by mood as well as by date.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">03</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>How The Dates Work</h3>
<p>Because tshechus follow the lunar calendar, the dates move every year, and the festival lasts three to five days. Start planning early: hotels and domestic flights around the big festivals fill months ahead, and the best seats in the courtyard go to those who arrive first. Tell us the month you hope to travel and we will line up the right festival with the rest of your itinerary.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">04</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>What To Wear And Bring</h3>
<ul class="tour-highlights tour-highlights--stack"><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Modest, comfortable clothing: shoulders and knees covered, and layers for cold mornings</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Sun hat and sunscreen: the courtyard has little shade at midday</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> A small cushion or folding stool for long hours on stone or wooden steps</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Water and snacks, since you may be seated for several hours at a time</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> A camera with a zoom lens, used discreetly from the edges of the courtyard</li></ul></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">05</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Watching With Respect</h3>
<p>Arrive early, sit where your guide suggests, and never step onto the dance ground. Take photographs quietly and never from in front of locals who are praying. Keep your voice low during the dances, and enjoy the <em>atsaras</em>, the clowns who move between the masked acts and bring laughter to the crowd. If you are invited to share food or a blessing from the families around you, accept with both hands and a smile.</p></div></li>
</ol>
<p>A tshechu works best when it sits inside a longer private journey, with time before and after to adjust to the altitude and see the valley around it. Our <a href="/tours/tsechu-festival-journey" class="faq-contact-link">Tsechu Festival Journey</a> is built around exactly that, and you can browse the full <a href="/festival-tours" class="faq-contact-link">festival tours</a> page for more.</p>', seo_description = 'A practical guide to Bhutan''s tshechu festivals: what they are, which ones to visit, how the dates work, what to wear and how to watch with respect.', updated_at = datetime('now')
  WHERE slug = 'a-traveler-s-guide-to-bhutan-s-tshechu-festivals' AND body_html = '';

UPDATE posts SET body_html = '<p>Bumthang is the part of Bhutan that people speak about softly. Four connected valleys at around 2,600 to 3,000 meters, scattered with ancient temples, farmhouses, apple orchards and pine forest, it feels unhurried in a way even Bhutan''s other valleys do not. Six days is a generous amount of time to give it. Here is how a slow week there tends to unfold.</p>
<ol class="article-list">
<li><span class="article-list-number">01</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Days One And Two: Arriving, And The Chokhor Valley</h3>
<p>Most journeys reach Bumthang by crossing the Yotong La, a high pass whose forest of rhododendron and prayer flags is a good first lesson in the region. After a settling-in afternoon, the Chokhor valley is the place to begin: the white walls of Jakar Dzong above town, the seventh-century Jambay Lhakhang, and the temple of Kurje, where Guru Rinpoche is said to have left the imprint of his body in a rock.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">02</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Day Three: Tamshing And A Slow Walk</h3>
<p>Tamshing Lhakhang, founded in the early 1500s by the treasure-revealer Pema Lingpa, holds some of the oldest murals in the country and a heavy chain-mail coat that visitors can carry around the temple for merit. Afterward, walk the footpaths between farmhouses; you will pass buckwheat fields, apple orchards and stone water-mills, with a monastery on every ridge.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">03</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Day Four: The Tang Valley And The Burning Lake</h3>
<p>The Tang valley is wilder and quieter. At Mebar Tsho, the Burning Lake, a gorge pool beneath a cliff face, Pema Lingpa is said to have retrieved hidden treasures and left a lasting blessing. Offer a small butter lamp, take a cup of tea, and stay as long as you like. Tang''s farmhouses and small guesthouses are good places to meet local families.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">04</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Day Five: Weavers And The Table</h3>
<p>Chume, known for its yathra woollen weaving, is a morning for watching loom work and, if you like, trying a few passes yourself. Bumthang has its own food traditions: buckwheat pancakes called <em>puta</em> and noodles, local cheese, honey and apple products. Our guides know the family kitchens worth stopping at, and we are happy to arrange a home-cooked lunch.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">05</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Day Six: Departure Without Hurry</h3>
<p>Leave time for one last quiet moment: early prayers at a temple, a final walk, or simply a long breakfast looking at the valley. Whether you fly out from Bathpalathang or drive on toward Trongsa and the west, the road itself is part of the journey, and we plan it with plenty of room to stop.</p></div></li>
</ol>
<p>Bumthang rewards the traveler who stays a little longer than planned. Our <a href="/tours/bumthang-spiritual-trail" class="faq-contact-link">Bumthang Spiritual Trail</a> follows much of this route, and we can stretch it, shorten it or combine it with the east of the country to suit your pace.</p>', seo_description = 'A six-day diary of Bumthang, Bhutan''s spiritual heartland: Jakar, Jambay Lhakhang, Kurje, Tamshing, the Burning Lake, Ura, Chume weaving, cheese and buckwheat pancakes.', updated_at = datetime('now')
  WHERE slug = 'six-days-in-bumthang-a-valley-diary' AND body_html = '';

UPDATE posts SET body_html = '<p>Bhutan''s treks climb quickly, from warm valleys to cold passes above 4,000 meters, and the weather can shift in an afternoon. The good news is that you do not need a great deal of gear to be comfortable: a small, well-chosen kit and a layered approach are enough. Here is the list our guides give guests before every trek.</p>
<ol class="article-list">
<li><span class="article-list-number">01</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Footwear And Feet</h3>
<ul class="tour-highlights tour-highlights--stack"><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Broken-in, waterproof hiking boots with ankle support and a firm sole</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Wool or synthetic socks, at least three pairs, plus a lighter pair for camp</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Camp shoes or sandals for the evenings</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Gaiters, useful in wet or snowy conditions on higher passes</li></ul></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">02</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Clothing In Layers</h3>
<ul class="tour-highlights tour-highlights--stack"><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Moisture-wicking base layers for the day, and a warm thermal set for sleeping</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> A fleece or light insulated jacket, and a warm down jacket for camp</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Waterproof jacket and trousers that you are happy to wear all day if it rains</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Trekking trousers that dry quickly, and a hat, gloves and buff for the passes</li></ul></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">03</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Sleeping And Carrying</h3>
<p>Our crew provides tents, mattresses and a camp kitchen, and your main bag travels on pack animals. Bring a good sleeping bag, comfortable to roughly &minus;10&deg;C for higher routes (we can arrange rentals), and a daypack of around 25 to 30 liters for what you need during the walking day. Keep your main duffel soft-sided and packed in dry bags, as animals carry limited weight.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">04</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Sun, Water And Health</h3>
<ul class="tour-highlights tour-highlights--stack"><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Sunglasses, a wide-brimmed hat, and strong sunscreen and lip balm for high altitude</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> A refillable water bottle or bladder; your crew provides safe boiled water</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Trekking poles, which protect the knees on long descents</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> A personal first-aid kit with blister care, and any medication you take regularly</li><li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" /></svg> Speak to your doctor about altitude before you travel, especially for routes above 4,000 meters</li></ul></div></li>
<li><span class="article-list-number">05</span><div class="article-list-content"><h3>Small Things That Matter</h3>
<p>A headlamp with spare batteries, a power bank, a light towel, wet wipes, a small personal toilet kit for the trail, and a notebook. Leave non-essentials in Thimphu: we store a spare bag at the hotel while you trek. Pack light, keep your daypack organized, and everything else becomes easy.</p></div></li>
</ol>
<p>Not sure which trek suits you? A short route like the <a href="/tours/druk-path-trek" class="faq-contact-link">Druk Path Trek</a> is a good first step, and the <a href="/tours/jomolhari-trek" class="faq-contact-link">Jomolhari Trek</a> is a classic for those who want a bigger mountain. Tell us your fitness and your dates, and we will recommend the right one.</p>', seo_description = 'What to pack for a trek in Bhutan: layering, boots, sleeping bag, rain gear, sun protection, health basics and what Altara''s crew provides on the trail.', updated_at = datetime('now')
  WHERE slug = 'what-to-pack-for-a-bhutan-trek' AND body_html = '';
